---
import SeoLayout from '../../layouts/SeoLayout.astro';
import { personaBySlug, isPublished, personaUrl, guideUrl } from '../../lib/seo-routes';
import { APP_URL } from '../../lib/site';

const persona = personaBySlug('sales-teams');
const title = 'Live translation for sales teams';
const description =
  'The routine client calls that currently happen in approximate English, translated — with ' +
  'per-room glossaries for your product terms and pricing you can quote without a sales call.';
---

<SeoLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={personaUrl(persona.slug)}
  noindex={!isPublished(persona)}
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      Nobody books an interpreter for a Tuesday check-in. So those calls happen in whatever English
      both sides can manage, and the quality of the conversation is set by the weaker speaker rather
      than by who has the most to say.
    </p>

    <h2>The calls this is actually for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Recurring check-ins with an international account</li>
      <li>Discovery calls where the decision-maker is not the person who emails you</li>
      <li>Support and escalation calls where the customer is already frustrated</li>
      <li>Candidate interviews for roles that do not require fluency in your working language</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are competing with <strong>nothing</strong> — not with an interpreter — which changes the
      standard the tooling has to meet.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the native tooling usually does not fit</h2>
    <p>
      Client calls are external calls, and that is precisely where the platform features fall over.
      <strong>Teams Interpreter excludes external guests and anonymous users</strong> and requires Microsoft
      365 Copilot. <strong
        >Google Meet handles one English-anchored language pair per meeting</strong
      >, with a 90-minute cap. <strong>Zoom gives captions but no translated audio</strong>, which
      is poor in a negotiation where tone carries meaning.
    </p>

    <h2>What matters in practice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Per-room glossaries</strong> — product names, SKUs, plan names and contract terms fixed
        once and reused
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Published per-minute pricing</strong>, so a rep can start a call without raising a
        purchase request
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Credits that never expire</strong>, which suits irregular usage far better than
        prepaid hour blocks
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When you should not choose us</h2>
    <p>
      For the deal that decides your quarter, book a human interpreter — the judgement in a
      negotiation is worth more than the convenience. And if your buyer's procurement requires SOC 2
      Type II and ISO 27001 today, we do not have them, and an established vendor is the honest
      answer.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('sales-call-with-a-foreign-client')}
          >Running a sales call with a client who speaks another language</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('translate-a-client-call-in-real-time')}
          >How to translate a client call in real time</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={guideUrl('customer-support-call-in-another-language')}
          >Handling a customer support call in another language</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="cta">
      <a href={APP_URL}>Try it on your next client call</a> — no contract, no minimum.
    </p>
  </article>
</SeoLayout>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 72ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 3rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  ul {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
  li {
    margin-block: 0.5rem;
  }
  .cta {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
